{
  "question": "How did space telescopes change what astronomers could see, and what did each generation of instrument add?",
  "plan": {
    "sub_questions": [
      "What fundamental observational limits did ground-based telescopes have that space telescopes overcame (atmospheric absorption, turbulence, background)?",
      "Which wavelength ranges and physical processes did successive generations of space telescopes open up (ultraviolet, X\u2011ray, infrared, high\u2011energy)?",
      "How did later generations improve key instrument metrics such as angular resolution, sensitivity, and spectroscopic capability, and what new science those improvements enabled?"
    ],
    "phrasings": [
      "How did space telescopes change what astronomers could see, and what did each generation of instrument add?",
      "Space telescopes removed the blurring and absorption of Earth's atmosphere and revealed finer detail and previously hidden wavelengths.",
      "Early space observatories opened the ultraviolet and high-energy windows that are inaccessible from the ground.",
      "Later generations of space telescopes added infrared capability to see through dust and probe the cold and distant universe.",
      "Each new generation improved angular resolution and sensitivity and brought more capable spectrometers for physical diagnostics.",
      "Space observatories transformed our view by exposing energetic X\u2011ray and gamma\u2011ray phenomena as well as faint, redshifted galaxies.",
      "Over time successive instruments built on earlier gains, expanding wavelength coverage, depth, and image quality."
    ],
    "visual_phrasings": [
      "Side\u2011by\u2011side comparison of the same astronomical object taken from the ground and from space showing increased sharpness and wavelength coverage.",
