  "answer": {
    "answer": "The archive shows a clear progression: early space observatories like Skylab moved observations off Earth and began seeing the Sun in new ways [2].  Later space telescopes added new parts of the spectrum \u2014 Hubble provided high-resolution optical/UV views [3][13], Chandra opened X-ray views of high-energy objects [6][8], and Spitzer along with WISE/NEOWISE revealed an infrared universe of cool and dust\u2011enshrouded sources [7][10][1].  The collection also highlights a shift from single-object imaging toward wide surveys and time-domain searches (Kepler/TESS and NEOWISE) that enabled statistical discovery of exoplanets and asteroids [12][10].  In sum, each generation added new wavelengths and survey modes that let astronomers see phenomena invisible to earlier instruments [3][6][7].",
    "citations": [
      1,
      2,
      3,
      6,
      7,
      8,
      10,
      12,
      13
    ],
    "chronology": [
      {
        "citations": [
          1
        ],
        "claim": "The archive includes early space-era material that predates later dedicated telescopes and shows the move to space-based observing that set the stage for later multiwavelength missions.",
        "era": 1965
      }
    ],
